rse_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7eec79cfad6a2a5967094f14d52db2086f21266b4a158eabf392549f95d0903"} {"question" "q-73151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aa05f8c056e95e2a6435d78375d3d8cf4b48e1270110dca7e56a0406ab180fd0"} {"question" "q-73151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7eec79cfad6a2a5967094f14d52db2086f21266b4a158eabf392549f95d0903"} {"question" "q-73151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"] ["text" "${a}_{32}$ only"] ["text" "${a}_{32}$ only ${a}_{33}$ only"] ["text" "${a}_{32},$ ${a}_{33},$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"] ["text" "${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7eec79cfad6a2a5967094f14d52db2086f21266b4a158eabf392549f95d0903"} {"question" "q-73151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${a}_{32},$ ${a}_{33},$ and ${a}_{41}$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7eec79cfad6a2a5967094f14d52db2086f21266b4a158eabf392549f95d0903"} {"question" "e-6166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/a29e80b35c9505ee665a109978de9b936ee7ce59eef631d7af9a4b7954bd8696.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-6166.json" "file_sha256" "c4b8485085b1c853988b18fd534afdc41594dce2b022c05e48b037c889cf2b77"} {"question" "e-6166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/d2f89fe83787daf667e01fd4c4fe644bc36cfe23b046088c22e8de17bb02dc28.png)\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-6166.json" "file_sha256" "c4b8485085b1c853988b18fd534afdc41594dce2b022c05e48b037c889cf2b77"} {"question" "e-4688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/e300a9ac18f0170ae9053c9635fe13a9dd6def793ebfc0bd9dbe81e28810a347.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-4688.json" "file_sha256" "9fce6ff0252ee19f5c448d1cb1d60a6f029a0fab2bba7c1c0bba792190997e76"} {"question" "e-4688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/f3d533c5687da1a75a07c6ad86b40ee8aa6ea5c86cdd250d5c47ff99b885ef61.png)\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/5d576fca72f6a4f9f0668ff0efbdc2cacc33f8dbb2c00192a7397da9ec83adea.png)\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/4f6a034def35760ae94b6eb6a425c59950556a78c124aa9ad0fcfdaa06053b28.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-4688.json" "file_sha256" "9fce6ff0252ee19f5c448d1cb1d60a6f029a0fab2bba7c1c0bba792190997e76"} {"question" "e-4687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/34184a95be2b3ae6865fde109ace7e8b4397f4b01982928be73cff4199792d61.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-4687.json" "file_sha256" "71c379b2882feb29f833b1c31675f486d4f79adc8a54ce6ace918aa5b791ec5a"} {"question" "e-4687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/b9f56d3f0414f4b56b50e59c2c02d9072f1fb74ce09d801a1ab62abac61ad737.png)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/8562a693df46625ee0dc2bcdecc664dfc50a99889fdb855ae2e06dc1db02a3d4.png)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/d263bfe2386309c55f1d63d97d15eaa682e9b0748a9bee6d3378d0b9035927ba.png)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/6ccb00b5e1387cd15847c50d66b26ee903d95428bcff2bfc933b675f9b590717.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-4687.json" "file_sha256" "71c379b2882feb29f833b1c31675f486d4f79adc8a54ce6ace918aa5b791ec5a"} {"question" "e-4689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/085cb01a2e4da286ea19a6f1e1707e685646af3d887abb1a57ab50e4d352c53f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-4689.json" "file_sha256" "4ed490c5200266dd62a7bf530097111e3a23315cb1e327257512a1e0bed6cd78"} {"question" "e-4689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/assets/d7b93aa8abed895e49b965b8d0905088249475cad98757e7a93a174fae143b49.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071571/example-4689.json" "file_sha256" "4ed490c5200266dd62a7bf530097111e3a23315cb1e327257512a1e0bed6cd78"}] "usage" {"q-73148" [] "q-129232" [] "q-129423" [] "q-73151" [] "q-42008" [] "e-6166" [] "e-4687" [] "e-4689" [] "q-42006" [] "e-4688" []} "basis" 2568 "retractions" [[17592186129708 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186128930 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186128930 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"] [17592186128863 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128863 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128862 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128862 :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128864 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186128864 :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186128043 17592186128044 17592186128045 17592186128046 17592186128047 17592186304455 17592186304456 17592186304457 17592186304458 17592186304459 17592186304462 17592186304463 17592186304464 17592186304465 17592186304466 17592186304468 17592186304469 17592186304470 17592186304471 17592186304472 17592186304524 17592186304525 17592186304526 17592186304527 17592186304528 17592186304531 17592186304532 17592186304533 17592186304534 17592186304535] "immutable_fields" [[17592186128042 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128042 :answer-field/id] [17592186128042 :answer-field/key] [17592186128042 :answer-field/type] [17592186304454 :answer-field/correct] [17592186304454 :answer-field/id] [17592186304454 :answer-field/key] [17592186304454 :answer-field/type] [17592186304461 :answer-field/correct] [17592186304461 :answer-field/id] [17592186304461 :answer-field/key] [17592186304461 :answer-field/type] [17592186304467 :answer-field/correct] [17592186304467 :answer-field/id] [17592186304467 :answer-field/key] [17592186304467 :answer-field/type] [17592186304523 :answer-field/correct] [17592186304523 :answer-field/id] [17592186304523 :answer-field/key] [17592186304523 :answer-field/type] [17592186304530 :answer-field/correct] [17592186304530 :answer-field/id] [17592186304530 :answer-field/key] [17592186304530 :an